WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:32:02.487 --> 00:32:08.793
♪ organ music playing ♪

00:32:20.872 --> 00:32:23.341
Hello everyone!

00:32:23.341 --> 00:32:26.978
To first-year, transfer, and
Eli Whitney students:

00:32:26.978 --> 00:32:28.680
Welcome to Yale!

00:32:28.680 --> 00:32:33.151
I also extend a warm welcome
to all the family members
and friends who

00:32:33.151 --> 00:32:38.122
join me in recognizing this
milestone in your lives.

00:32:38.122 --> 00:32:41.426
Today we begin
a new academic year

00:32:41.426 --> 00:32:44.829
at a time of
significant upheaval.

00:32:44.829 --> 00:32:51.002
Historically, social conflict
and disease pandemics
have catalyzed change.

00:32:51.002 --> 00:32:54.072
And so we should not be
surprised that many are

00:32:54.072 --> 00:32:59.143
calling now for further
transformation of our society.

00:32:59.143 --> 00:33:03.247
We are dealing with two
parallel crises:

00:33:03.247 --> 00:33:05.917
The COVID-19 pandemic 

00:33:05.917 --> 00:33:09.821
and the racial
inequality and injustice

00:33:09.821 --> 00:33:15.893
rooted in our country's
history of slavery and
still facing us today.

00:33:15.893 --> 00:33:19.097
We have all been affected by
these challenges,

00:33:19.097 --> 00:33:26.471
especially members of the Black
and brown communities who bear
the brunt of these burdens

00:33:26.471 --> 00:33:31.042
I have been giving a lot of
thought to the university's role

00:33:31.042 --> 00:33:34.479
in addressing 
difficult social issues.

00:33:34.479 --> 00:33:37.815
Much remains to be answered
in this turbulent period,

00:33:37.815 --> 00:33:45.790
but what is certain is that the
core mission of Yale is
more relevant than ever.

00:33:45.790 --> 00:33:51.229
I am sure each of you
has studied Yale's
mission statement:

00:33:51.229 --> 00:33:56.868
Yale is comitteded to
improving the world today
and for future generations

00:33:56.868 --> 00:34:04.642
through outstanding research
and scholarship, education,
preservation, and practice.

00:34:04.642 --> 00:34:12.050
Yale educates aspiring leaders
worldwide who serve all
sectors of society.

00:34:12.050 --> 00:34:16.054
We carry out
this mission through the
free exchange of ideas

00:34:16.054 --> 00:34:20.291
in an ethical, interdependent,
and diverse community

00:34:20.291 --> 00:34:26.030
of faculty, staff,
students, and alumni.

00:34:26.030 --> 00:34:31.636
Research, scholarship,
education, preservation,
and practice are vital

00:34:31.636 --> 00:34:36.874
to understanding our past,
building on
hard earned progress,

00:34:36.874 --> 00:34:42.780
identifying what
still needs to be done,
and creating change.

00:34:42.780 --> 00:34:44.816
You know this;

00:34:44.816 --> 00:34:49.120
It is why you are here.

00:34:49.120 --> 00:34:53.691
You will be studying with
professors who use their
knowledge and expertise

00:34:53.691 --> 00:34:55.960
to improve the world.

00:34:55.960 --> 00:34:59.730
Yale faculty members work with
government officials and others

00:34:59.730 --> 00:35:03.301
on containing the spread
of infectious diseases,

00:35:03.301 --> 00:35:07.638
mitigating health disparities,
transforming policing,

00:35:07.638 --> 00:35:11.776
and reforming the
criminal justice system.

00:35:11.776 --> 00:35:16.981
While addressing challenges
like these, you will learn
from your teachers

00:35:16.981 --> 00:35:19.517
how to analyze problems,

00:35:19.517 --> 00:35:21.419
think critically,

00:35:21.419 --> 00:35:24.188
and communicate clearly.

00:35:24.188 --> 00:35:32.096
Along the way,
you will also be encouraged
to focus on yourself.

00:35:32.096 --> 00:35:36.267
Whether fighting a
global pandemic or
the scourge of racism,

00:35:36.267 --> 00:35:39.937
global climate change
or economic recession,

00:35:39.937 --> 00:35:48.246
we must be willing to be
honest about our own motivations
and open to changing our minds.

00:35:48.246 --> 00:35:52.450
Efforts to solve
pressing problems
will not be effective

00:35:52.450 --> 00:35:59.724
if they are led by individuals
who cannot feel compassion
nor learn to cooperate,

00:35:59.724 --> 00:36:07.798
nor by those who try to achieve
change through manipulation,
coercion, or brute force.

00:36:07.798 --> 00:36:11.135
Yale's faculty understands this.

00:36:11.135 --> 00:36:18.676
I recently discussed compassion
with Sterling Professor of
Sociology Elijah Anderson.

00:36:18.676 --> 00:36:25.750
He has contributed immensely to
the study of racial inequality,
particularly in urban settings,

00:36:25.750 --> 00:36:29.187
but he also has put his
expertise into practice

00:36:29.187 --> 00:36:33.925
as a consultant to the
White House and to Congress.

00:36:33.925 --> 00:36:41.065
I asked him,
how does he teach students who
want to improve the world?

00:36:41.065 --> 00:36:47.171
He told me that he shows his
students how to see the world
from the point of view of others

00:36:47.171 --> 00:36:49.674
how to have empathy.

00:36:49.674 --> 00:36:55.713
Such an ability, he noted,
can help us to be
better scholars and students,

00:36:55.713 --> 00:37:02.019
to be more sensitive to the
human condition, and to
be more self-aware.

00:37:02.019 --> 00:37:07.525
When we are
motivated by empathy,
compassion, and cooperation,

00:37:07.525 --> 00:37:09.927
we can bridge differences,

00:37:09.927 --> 00:37:12.263
learn to understand one another,

00:37:12.263 --> 00:37:17.034
and enact meaningful,
sustainable change.

00:37:17.034 --> 00:37:20.071
Empathy and compassion, 

00:37:20.071 --> 00:37:24.475
a willingness to engage those
with whom we may not see
eye-to-eye

00:37:24.475 --> 00:37:27.812
and a concern for the
misfortunes of others,

00:37:27.812 --> 00:37:31.182
are preconditions
for cooperation.

00:37:31.182 --> 00:37:35.319
Cooperation, working together
to solve a common problem,

00:37:35.319 --> 00:37:40.124
especially one that we cannot
overcome by working alone,

00:37:40.124 --> 00:37:44.695
requires us to listen to
each other charitably,

00:37:44.695 --> 00:37:47.598
appreciate
each other's perspective,

00:37:47.598 --> 00:37:51.402
and inhibit our desire to
dismiss individuals

00:37:51.402 --> 00:37:58.109
with whom we initially believe 
we cannot develop
common purpose.

00:37:58.109 --> 00:38:05.216
The enemies of
compassion and cooperation
are fear and anger.

00:38:05.216 --> 00:38:08.886
Now I'm not saying that it is
inappropriate to experience

00:38:08.886 --> 00:38:15.059
the fear caused by a pandemic of
a novel virus that can be fatal.

00:38:15.059 --> 00:38:19.964
And I'm not saying that one
shouldn't feel anger,
indeed, outrage,

00:38:19.964 --> 00:38:27.405
at the killing of George Floyd
by former officers of the
Minneapolis police department.

00:38:27.405 --> 00:38:32.009
In fact, COVID-19
makes me anxious;

00:38:32.009 --> 00:38:37.682
and those former officers,
who took a sworn oath to
protect the public,

00:38:37.682 --> 00:38:41.085
they leave me livid.

00:38:41.085 --> 00:38:45.856
But although such fear
and anger can be motivating,

00:38:45.856 --> 00:38:53.030
I must ensure that they do not
prevent my finding common ground
with well-meaning others

00:38:53.030 --> 00:38:59.103
whose approaches to social
challenges are not
the same as mine.

00:38:59.103 --> 00:39:03.774
I must engage them rather than
attempt to silence them

00:39:03.774 --> 00:39:07.945
to accomplish anything
real and lasting.

00:39:07.945 --> 00:39:11.182
Let me provide an
example from politics.

00:39:11.182 --> 00:39:14.085
At one time,
women who became pregnant

00:39:14.085 --> 00:39:17.988
and men who wanted to take time
off from work to care for
an infant

00:39:17.988 --> 00:39:21.692
were at risk of being
fired by their employers.

00:39:21.692 --> 00:39:26.897
But in 1993,
the U.S. Senate passed the
Family and Medical Leave Act,

00:39:26.897 --> 00:39:31.435
guaranteeing family members the
ability to take a
prolonged unpaid leave

00:39:31.435 --> 00:39:35.673
without risking their jobs in
order to tend to a newborn,

00:39:35.673 --> 00:39:40.277
adopt a child,
or assist a sick family member.

00:39:40.277 --> 00:39:45.783
How did this act,
which was considered revolutionary
at the time, pass?

00:39:45.783 --> 00:39:48.586
Well, it was
developed cooperatively

00:39:48.586 --> 00:39:52.757
by Senator Ted Kennedy,
among the most liberal
of lawmakers,

00:39:52.757 --> 00:39:57.194
and Senator Orrin Hatch,
among the most conservative.

00:39:57.194 --> 00:40:00.064
They opposed one another
on many policies,

00:40:00.064 --> 00:40:05.803
but in this case, they found
a common cause:
supporting families.

00:40:05.803 --> 00:40:12.276
They both felt compassion toward
individuals at risk for losing
their jobs due to childbirth,

00:40:12.276 --> 00:40:18.115
and, perhaps more important,
they respected each other
as individuals,

00:40:18.115 --> 00:40:19.884
listened to each other,

00:40:19.884 --> 00:40:25.122
and placed national interest
ahead of party differences.

00:40:25.122 --> 00:40:32.730
Their cooperation led,
ultimately, to significant
social change.

00:40:32.730 --> 00:40:38.202
One final thought
about the importance of
compassion and cooperation:

00:40:38.202 --> 00:40:42.573
Most of you, members of the
Class of 2024,

00:40:42.573 --> 00:40:48.179
are part of Generation Z,
the post-millennials.

00:40:48.179 --> 00:40:51.882
I am, well, a Baby Boomer.

00:40:51.882 --> 00:40:55.886
Perhaps you have heard that you
and I are quite different.

00:40:55.886 --> 00:40:58.923
True, you are the
first “digital natives"

00:40:58.923 --> 00:41:01.392
and the only world
you have ever known

00:41:01.392 --> 00:41:06.363
has included the smartphone,
texting, and social media.

00:41:06.363 --> 00:41:09.733
And you are distinctive
in other ways as well.

00:41:09.733 --> 00:41:15.506
Yours is the most racially and
ethnically diverse generation
in American history,

00:41:15.506 --> 00:41:19.176
and you also promise
to be the best educated.

00:41:19.176 --> 00:41:22.713
But it is not only that we are
different in these ways;

00:41:22.713 --> 00:41:25.416
we also are said to disagree.

00:41:25.416 --> 00:41:31.689
We are told we have different
views on politics,
music, and work.

00:41:31.689 --> 00:41:38.229
I am sure you are familiar with
the stereotypes that
exaggerate our differences.

00:41:38.229 --> 00:41:43.501
And stereotyping is another
barrier to compassion
and cooperation.

00:41:43.501 --> 00:41:49.373
For example, you fault my
generation for the great
problems of our time.

00:41:49.373 --> 00:41:53.143
And my generation too often
fails to understand you.

00:41:53.143 --> 00:41:58.616
We pass you on the sidewalk,
mystified by your earbuds
and your clothes.

00:41:58.616 --> 00:42:03.087
We tell you to grow up,
to get off social media,

00:42:03.087 --> 00:42:06.390
to develop some
real-life coping skills,

00:42:06.390 --> 00:42:10.394
and to get out of your parents'
basements and get a job.

00:42:10.394 --> 00:42:15.065
And to us you say,
“OK, Boomer”

00:42:15.065 --> 00:42:20.371
Thanks to an article I read in
the Wall Street Journal,
a print edition of it, in fact,

00:42:20.371 --> 00:42:28.178
I am told this is a
“sly linguistic weapon
of intergenerational warfare."

00:42:28.178 --> 00:42:33.584
Well, although such stereotypes
give rise to internet memes and

00:42:33.584 --> 00:42:40.591
other kinds of clickbait to get
us to think of our differences
as “intergenerational warfare,"

00:42:40.591 --> 00:42:43.794
we are not at war.

00:42:43.794 --> 00:42:45.396
Those are distractions,

00:42:45.396 --> 00:42:48.566
and they won't help us link
arms in overcoming

00:42:48.566 --> 00:42:54.872
what might be standing in
the way of our future,
our shared future.

00:42:54.872 --> 00:43:02.046
At the heart of "OK, Boomer" is
the belief that my generation
contributed to the serious,

00:43:02.046 --> 00:43:10.020
even existential,
problems in the world that you
will enter and lead.

00:43:10.020 --> 00:43:15.693
Your generation sees the
challenges posed by
climate change, disease,

00:43:15.693 --> 00:43:21.098
abusive policing, war,
racism, and poverty.

00:43:21.098 --> 00:43:25.169
You recognize the
urgency of these problems.

00:43:25.169 --> 00:43:29.273
But my generation does as well.

00:43:29.273 --> 00:43:35.946
Instead of dismissing one
another's perspectives and
focusing only on difference,

00:43:35.946 --> 00:43:43.120
we need to direct our attention
to creating something better
for all who will follow us.

00:43:43.120 --> 00:43:49.593
We can only do that
by harnessing our compassion
and cooperating.

00:43:49.593 --> 00:43:53.864
Here at Yale,
we learn from each other.

00:43:53.864 --> 00:44:00.037
That is why academics like me
enjoy spending their entire
lives on college campuses.

00:44:00.037 --> 00:44:04.174
And it is why you
chose to come to Yale.

00:44:04.174 --> 00:44:07.945
The passing of knowledge and
the responsibility to
do some good with it

00:44:07.945 --> 00:44:13.317
from one generation to the next
is a large part
of Yale's mission.

00:44:13.317 --> 00:44:15.552
It is hard work.

00:44:15.552 --> 00:44:20.591
So, please:
use your time at Yale wisely.

00:44:20.591 --> 00:44:24.094
Let us devote ourselves to
learning together,

00:44:24.094 --> 00:44:31.669
to the personal transformation
that the pursuit of knowledge
can create for each of us.

00:44:31.669 --> 00:44:36.040
Let us use these college years
to change ourselves,

00:44:36.040 --> 00:44:40.044
to become more
compassionate human beings.

00:44:40.044 --> 00:44:43.080
And then let us
work cooperatively

00:44:43.080 --> 00:44:46.216
on existing and
emerging challenges

00:44:46.216 --> 00:44:49.920
to create the world we desire.

00:44:49.920 --> 00:44:55.025
Welcome to Yale!

